# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment configuration — TEMPLATE
#
# This file is committed to the repository and contains NO real secrets.
# Copy it to `.env` and put the real values there. `.env` is listed in
# .gitignore and must never be committed.
#
#   Windows PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   macOS / Linux:       cp .env.example .env
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# TWO database accounts, deliberately. This is the single most important safety
# arrangement in the system — see Step 3 of docs/build-plan.md.
#
# 1. The RESTRICTED account the application runs as. Not a superuser, cannot bypass
#    Row-Level Security, owns no tables. PostgreSQL ignores those walls entirely for
#    superusers and for a table's owner, so the application must be neither.
#    Created by `npm run db:setup-roles`, which reads the name and password from here.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://gestionale_app:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/gestionale_palestre?schema=public"

# 2. The PRIVILEGED account, used ONLY to run migrations. Never used to serve a request.
DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL="postgresql://postgres:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/gestionale_palestre?schema=public"

# Signs the cookie that keeps somebody logged in. Must be a long random value, and a
# DIFFERENT one on every installation — anyone holding it could forge a login.
# Generate one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
AUTH_SECRET="CHANGE_ME"

# Sending email (password resets), through Resend — https://resend.com
# LEAVE THIS AS IT IS FOR DEVELOPMENT: with no real key, messages are printed in the
# terminal instead of being sent, so nothing depends on an external service.
# To send for real: create a Resend account, generate an API key, paste it here.
RESEND_API_KEY="CHANGE_ME"
EMAIL_FROM="Gestionale Palestre <onboarding@resend.dev>"
